 ### Quaternion Fractal Generation
-A fractal distance function based on quaternion iteration, similar to Mandelbrot sets but in 3D [S2, unofficial]. It uses quaternion algebra to iterate points in 4D space, projecting to 3D [S2, unofficial]. Initialization sets $v = (p_x, p_y, p_z, 0.0)$ and derivative $d = (1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)$ [S2, unofficial]. The loop runs $n$ from $1$ to $ITERATIONS-1$, updating $d = 2.0 \times (v_x d_x - \text{dot}(v_{yzw}, d_{yzw}), v_x d_{yzw} + d_x v_{yzw} + \text{cross}(v_{yzw}, d_{yzw}))$ and $v = (v_x^2 - \text{dot}(v_{yzw}, v_{yzw}), 2.0 v_x v_{yzw}) + c$ [S2, unofficial]. Divergence is checked via $\text{dot}(v, v) > 10.0$ [S2, unofficial]. The final distance estimate is $0.5 \times r \times \log(r) / \|d\|$, where $r = \|v\|$ [S2, unofficial]. Parameters are $ITERATIONS = 5$ and $c = (0.18, 0.88, 0.24, 0.16)$ [S2, unofficial].
+A fractal distance function based on quaternion iteration, similar to Mandelbrot sets but in 3D [S2, unofficial].
+**Steps:**
+1. Initialize $v = \text{vec4}(p, 0.0)$ and $d = \text{vec4}(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0)$.
+2. Loop $n$ from $1$ to $ITERATIONS-1$:
+   Update $d = 2.0 \times \text{vec4}(v.x \cdot d.x - \text{dot}(v.xzw, d.yzw), v.x \cdot d.yzw + d.x \cdot v.yzw + \text{cross}(v.yzw, d.yzw))$.
+   Update $v = \text{vec4}(v.x^2 - \text{dot}(v.yzw, v.yzw), \text{vec3}(2.0 \cdot v.x \cdot v.yzw)) + c$.
+3. Check divergence: if $\text{dot}(v, v) > 10.0$, break loop.
+**Formula:** Final distance: $0.5 \times r \times \log(r) / \|d\|$ where $r = \|v\|$.
+**Parameters:** $ITERATIONS = 5$; $c = \text{vec4}(0.18, 0.88, 0.24, 0.16)$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{\text{max}})$ per voxel, where $N_{\text{max}}$ is the maximum iteration count [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space per voxel query [G].
-*   **Performance:** The use of trigonometric functions (`sin`, `cos`, `acos`, `atan2`) makes each iteration computationally expensive compared to polynomial-only fractals [G]. Typical default parameters ($N_{\text{max}}=5, E=8$) are relatively fast, but increasing iterations or power significantly increases render time [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Produces highly detailed, organic-looking 3D fractal structures with self-similarity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High computational cost per voxel. The escape radius and power parameter heavily influence the visual result and convergence speed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When generating static 3D fractal models or high-quality offline renders [G].
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is critical and simpler noise functions suffice [G].
+Time complexity is $O(N_{max})$ per voxel. Memory complexity is $O(1)$ per voxel evaluation. Performance scales linearly with iteration count. The method is computationally intensive due to trigonometric functions and power operations. It is suitable for static fractal generation but may be slow for real-time ray marching without optimization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The Mandelbulb is a 3D fractal discovered in 2009 by Daniel Shapira and others, inspired by the 2D Mandelbrot set [G]. It extends the concept of complex number iteration to 3D using spherical coordinates [S1, unofficial]. The method described here follows the implementation details found in procedural fractal generation code [S1, unofficial]. It is used in computer graphics for generating complex, natural-looking 3D shapes and textures [S1, unofficial].
+Originated from the holographic fractals project [S1, unofficial]. The Mandelbulb is a 3D analogue of the Mandelbrot set, constructed using polar coordinate transformations [S1, unofficial]. It is used in procedural geometry generation and voxel art [S1, unofficial].
 
